--- a/CHANGELOG
+++ b/CHANGELOG
@@ -28,9 +28,56 @@ Security Fixes
 Enhancements
 
 * Modernize type annotations in top-level API functions using PEP 585 and PEP 604 syntax.
-
-Bug Fixes
-
+* Document ``wrap_after`` as the column after which a list wraps rather than a
+  "column limit". Items are added until the column is passed, so the item that
+  crosses it stays on the line and the line ends past the column - the CLI help
+  already said this and the API docs did not.
+
+Bug Fixes
+
+* Fix ``Function.get_window()`` raising ``AttributeError`` on any function
+  without an ``OVER`` clause. The guard ``if not over_clause`` tested the tuple
+  ``token_next_by`` returns, and ``(None, None)`` is truthy, so it never fired
+  and the following line indexed ``None``. The method now unpacks the answer
+  and returns ``None`` when there is no window, which is what its docstring
+  already promised.
+
+* Fix ``IndexError`` when formatting a parenthesis that holds fewer than three
+  tokens. ``StripWhitespaceFilter._stripws_parenthesis`` read the second and
+  second-to-last children of a ``Parenthesis`` without checking it had that
+  many, but grouping does not guarantee the shape ``('(', body..., ')')``:
+  ``(as)`` comes back as a ``Parenthesis`` holding a single ``Identifier`` that
+  swallowed both parens. ``sqlparse.format('(as)', reindent=True)`` therefore
+  raised ``IndexError`` instead of returning, under ``strip_whitespace``,
+  ``reindent`` and ``reindent_aligned`` alike, and ``sqlformat`` exited 1 with a
+  traceback on a four-byte file. Every index into the token list is now bounded,
+  including the nested read that popped from a group until it could empty it.
+
+* Fix ``output_format`` emitting a snippet that does not match the SQL it was
+  given. Three defects, all in ``sqlparse/filters/output.py``:
+
+  - The parentheses that make a multi-line Python assignment legal were decided
+    from the stripped statement text, so a script written one statement per line
+    produced continuation lines with no parentheses around them - unparseable
+    Python for input as ordinary as ``select 1;\nselect 2;``.
+
+  - Only the first newline of a whitespace run started a new line, so the rest
+    stayed inside the open literal. In Python that is invalid syntax; in PHP the
+    remaining newlines silently escaped the ``.=`` continuation shape.
+
+  - Each token was read from its cached ``value``, which the filters that run
+    earlier leave stale when they edit inside a group. ``reindent`` and
+    ``reindent_aligned`` were therefore ignored in the emitted snippet whenever
+    their edits fell inside a group, and the snippet carried the unformatted
+    SQL.
+
+* Fix ``output_format="python"`` emitting invalid Python when the SQL carries a
+  raw control character inside a quoted token or a comment. A newline or
+  carriage return ended the generated string literal and a NUL made the whole
+  snippet unparseable; they are now written as ``\n``, ``\r`` and ``\x00``.
+  Such a character is not whitespace to the lexer, so it never reached the
+  serializer's line-splitting branch. PHP output is unchanged, since a
+  double-quoted PHP string carries these characters literally.
 * Fix statement splitting (issue845).
 * Fix a late-binding closure bug in `TokenList.token_not_matching`.
 * Fix function grouping being skipped in ``CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT``
@@ -45,6 +92,120 @@ Bug Fixes
   when it is followed by a float literal written without a leading zero
   (e.g. ``x BETWEEN .03 AND .06``), so the bounds are parsed as number tokens
   (issue601).
+* Fix ``reindent`` not being idempotent on multi-statement SQL. Each statement
+  after the first was given a blank line in front of it without the gap it
+  already had being removed, so re-formatting an already-formatted script grew
+  the gap by one newline per pass until it saturated at three - churn for any
+  format-on-save or pre-commit setup. The inter-statement gap is now
+  normalized, so it no longer grows from pass to pass.
+* Fix ``format`` deleting the space between two statements that share a line.
+  ``sqlparse.format('select 1; select 2;')`` returned ``'select 1;select 2;'``,
+  while the same statements separated by a newline came back untouched.
+  Trailing whitespace is invisible only at the end of a line of the document,
+  which no single statement can decide - the next one may start on that line -
+  so the statements are now joined before their lines are right-stripped
+  rather than each being stripped on its own. End-of-line and end-of-document
+  whitespace is still removed, and ``strip_whitespace`` still drains a
+  statement's trailing whitespace; that drain now removes all of it rather
+  than one token, which a stripped comment could leave behind as a pair.
+* Fix a doubled space, and a stray space at the start of a line, where a
+  comment ends a group. ``StripWhitespaceFilter`` asked each token whether it
+  was whitespace to decide if a separator was already in place, and a group's
+  own flag is False even when the group renders with a trailing space or
+  newline - as an ``Identifier`` does once a trailing comment is stripped out
+  of it. The space that followed the group was therefore kept as a second one,
+  which the next pass collapsed, so the output was not a fixed point either.
+* Fix ``reindent`` adding a blank line where a line comment had already ended
+  the line. ``reindent`` puts a line break before ``FROM``, ``AND``,
+  ``ORDER BY`` and the other clause keywords, and decided whether one was
+  already there by reading the single token in front of the keyword. When a
+  line comment ends that line the break lives inside the comment's own value
+  and the separator after it has already been emptied, so the token read
+  answered "no break here" and a second one was added. ``select foo``,
+  ``-- c``, `` from t`` came back with a blank line between the comment and
+  ``from``, and re-formatting that output removed it again, so the result was
+  not a fixed point. The indent that belongs with the keyword is still
+  applied, so ``AND`` inside a ``WHERE`` keeps its column.
+* Fix ``reindent`` dropping the line break before a clause keyword whose
+  separator it had just removed. ``reindent`` deletes the whitespace in front
+  of ``FROM``, ``AND``, ``OR`` and the rest and writes its own, and the break
+  it deletes can be one it inserted earlier in the same pass, and reading
+  the text before the keyword too early reported a break that had just
+  been removed. ``a,or`` came back as ``a,or``, with no break before the
+  ``OR`` that ``reindent`` exists to put there.
+* Reject ``reindent`` and ``reindent_aligned`` together. They are alternative
+  indentation styles, and enabling both stacked two indenters that each insert
+  a newline before the same keywords: the result was a blank line between
+  every clause, and it was unstable across passes because the aligned filter
+  deleted the inter-statement gap the other one had just inserted, gluing
+  statements together so the next pass parsed a different set of them.
+  ``sqlparse.format`` now raises ``SQLParseError`` for the combination.
+  ``indent_columns`` implies ``reindent``, so it collides with
+  ``reindent_aligned`` too.
+* Fix ``reindent_aligned`` raising ``ValueError`` instead of formatting when
+  a ``CASE`` has no ``END`` at its own level. A keyword that opens a group
+  takes everything after it, so in ``case where end`` the ``END`` sits inside
+  the ``WHERE``. The aligner looked for it without recursing and passed the
+  ``None`` it got back to ``token_index``, so ``sqlparse.format`` raised an
+  exception that is not ``SQLParseError`` and ``sqlformat -a`` printed a
+  traceback for a fourteen-character file. Such a ``CASE`` is now aligned
+  without aligning an ``END`` it does not have.
+* Fix ``identifier_case`` changing the case inside a quoted identifier. Case
+  inside quotes is part of the name, and ``SQL_REGEX`` gives four delimiters
+  an identifier token of their own - ``"..."``, ``` `...` ```, ``´...´`` and
+  ``[...]`` - but only the double quote was exempt. ``select `MyTbl` `` with
+  ``identifier_case="upper"`` became ``select `MYTBL` ``, which names a
+  different table on a case-sensitive MySQL, and ``capitalize`` lowercased
+  the whole name because the leading delimiter absorbed the capital.
+  Unquoted identifiers are folded exactly as before.
+* Fix ``sqlformat`` printing a traceback instead of an error message. Seven of
+  the twelve ways a run can fail crashed rather than reporting: SQL that trips a
+  documented grouping limit (both to stdout and in place), input bytes that do
+  not decode, an ``--encoding`` name no codec answers to (each of those last two
+  for both a named file and stdin), and output the console cannot encode. Every
+  one now emits the same single ``[ERROR] ...`` line and exits 1, so a failure
+  on one file still lets the remaining files be processed.
+* Fix ``sqlformat`` emptying files when formatting fails. ``--in-place`` and
+  ``-o`` opened the destination for writing - which truncates - before the
+  options were validated and the SQL was formatted, so any later failure left
+  the file at zero bytes. ``sqlformat --in-place --indent_width 0 f.sql``
+  emptied ``f.sql`` while reporting the invalid option, SQL exceeding a
+  grouping limit emptied it while raising, and with several files every file
+  was emptied. The text is now formatted before anything is opened for
+  writing, so a failing run leaves every file byte-identical.
+* Preserve trailing newlines. The statement splitter discarded a trailing
+  statement whose tokens were all whitespace, so any input ending in a
+  semicolon lost its final newline: ``parse("select 1;\n")`` round-tripped to
+  ``"select 1;"``. Trailing spaces were kept and inputs without a semicolon
+  were kept, so the loss was inconsistent as well as silent. The whitespace is
+  now attached to the statement it follows, which leaves ``split`` and the
+  number of parsed statements unchanged. ``sqlformat`` additionally restores a
+  file's final newline after reindenting, so ``--in-place`` no longer rewrites
+  the last byte of every file the pre-commit hook touches.
+* Fix the ``reindent_aligned`` validation error naming the wrong value. The
+  message interpolated ``reindent`` instead of ``reindent_aligned``, so
+  ``format(sql, reindent_aligned='x')`` reported ``Invalid value for
+  reindent_aligned: False`` - a value the caller never passed, and one that is
+  itself valid. With ``reindent=True`` also set it reported ``True``.
+* Remove the ``right_margin`` formatting option and ``RightMarginFilter``. The
+  option was validated as though it were supported and then raised a bare
+  ``NotImplementedError`` from a filter that had been disabled with a
+  "Doesn't work" note: with its ``keep_together`` list empty the filter split
+  qualified identifiers across line breaks, turning ``schema.table.column``
+  into three lines. It was never documented and no ``sqlformat`` flag exposed
+  it. Passing ``right_margin`` now raises ``SQLParseError`` instead of doing
+  nothing silently; ``right_margin=None`` remains a no-op.
+* Fix ``sqlformat --comma_first`` and ``--compact`` ignoring false values. Both
+  were declared ``type=bool``, which passes the raw string through ``bool()``,
+  so every non-empty value including ``False``, ``no`` and ``0`` switched the
+  option on. They now accept true/yes/y/on/1 and false/no/n/off/0 in any case
+  and reject anything else with an error instead of guessing.
+* Escape backslashes in ``output_format='python'`` and ``output_format='php'``.
+  Both serializers escaped the quote character but not the backslash, so SQL
+  containing a backslash produced a snippet that meant something else -
+  ``select 'a\nb'`` became a literal containing a real newline - and SQL ending
+  in a backslash produced an unterminated string literal. The PHP serializer
+  now also escapes ``$``, which PHP interpolates inside double-quoted strings.
 
 
